        prev, cur = 1, 1  # ways(i-1), ways(i)
        for _ in range(2, n + 1):
            prev, cur = cur, prev + cur
        return cur
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(1)
:::

**Pitfall:** The naive recursion times out around n ≈ 40. Always cache or iterate.

**Key idea:** Let `best(i)` be the cheapest way to reach position `i`. You arrive from `i−1` (paying `cost[i−1]`) or from `i−2` (paying `cost[i−2]`): `best(i) = min(best(i−1) + cost[i−1], best(i−2) + cost[i−2])`, with `best(0) = best(1) = 0`.

**Brute force:** Recursively try both jumps from every step without caching. Time O(2ⁿ), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Top-down DP (memoized recursion)
`best(i)` as defined above, cached. The answer is `best(n)`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] cost, memo;

    public int minCostClimbingStairs(int[] cost) {
        this.cost = cost;
        memo = new int[cost.length + 1];
        Arrays.fill(memo, -1);
        return best(cost.length);
    }

    private int best(int i) {
        if (i <= 1) return 0; // starting on step 0 or 1 is free
        if (memo[i] == -1) memo[i] = Math.min(best(i - 1) + cost[i - 1], best(i - 2) + cost[i - 2]);
        return memo[i];
    }
}
```

