:::

**Pitfall:** The top is index `n`, not `n − 1`. Returning `dp[n−1]` forces you to stand on (and pay for) the last step.

**Key idea:** At house `i` you either skip it (keep the best for the first `i` houses) or take it (its money plus the best up to `i − 2`): `best(i) = max(best(i−1), nums[i] + best(i−2))`.

**Brute force:** Try every subset of houses, discard those with neighbours, and take the max sum. Time O(2ⁿ·n), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Top-down DP (memoized recursion)
`best(i)` = most money from houses `0..i`. Recurse with the take/skip choice and cache each `i`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private int[] nums, memo;

    public int rob(int[] nums) {
        this.nums = nums;
        memo = new int[nums.length];
        Arrays.fill(memo, -1);
        return best(nums.length - 1);
    }

    private int best(int i) {
        if (i < 0) return 0;
        if (memo[i] == -1) memo[i] = Math.max(best(i - 1), nums[i] + best(i - 2)); // skip vs take
        return memo[i];
    }
}
```

