:::

:::details 3. Manacher's algorithm (linear)
Insert `#` between characters so every palindrome has odd length. Keep the rightmost palindrome found so far `[center − r, center + r]`. A position `i` inside it starts with the radius of its mirror `2·center − i` (capped at the boundary), then expands. Every expansion pushes the right boundary forward, so the total work is linear.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public String longestPalindrome(String s) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("^");
        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) sb.append('#').append(ch);
        char[] t = sb.append("#$").toString().toCharArray(); // ^ and $ are sentinels
        int[] p = new int[t.length];
        int center = 0, right = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < t.length - 1; i++) {
            if (i < right) p[i] = Math.min(right - i, p[2 * center - i]); // reuse the mirror
            while (t[i + p[i] + 1] == t[i - p[i] - 1]) p[i]++;
            if (i + p[i] > right) { center = i; right = i + p[i]; }
        }
        int best = 0, at = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < t.length - 1; i++)
            if (p[i] > best) { best = p[i]; at = i; }
        int start = (at - best - 1) / 2;
        return s.substring(start, start + best);
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
class Solution:
    def longestPalindrome(self, s: str) -> str:
        t = "^#" + "#".join(s) + "#$"  # ^ and $ are sentinels
        p = [0] * len(t)
        center = right = 0
        for i in range(1, len(t) - 1):
            if i < right:
                p[i] = min(right - i, p[2 * center - i])  # reuse the mirror
            while t[i + p[i] + 1] == t[i - p[i] - 1]:
                p[i] += 1
            if i + p[i] > right:
                center, right = i, i + p[i]
        best, at = max((r, i) for i, r in enumerate(p))
        start = (at - best - 1) // 2
        return s[start:start + best]
```

**Time:** O(n)
**Space:** O(n)
:::

**Pitfall:** Only expanding around characters misses even-length answers like `"bb"`.

**Key idea:** Each palindrome has a unique centre (a character or a gap). Expand from every centre; each successful expansion step is one more palindrome.

**Brute force:** Test every one of the n(n+1)/2 substrings with two pointers. Time O(n³), Space O(1).

:::details 1. Expand around centre
For each of the 2n − 1 centres, expand while the two ends match and add 1 per step.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    public int countSubstrings(String s) {
        int count = 0, n = s.length();
        for (int c = 0; c < 2 * n - 1; c++) { // even c = a character, odd c = a gap
            int l = c / 2, r = l + c % 2;
            while (l >= 0 && r < n && s.charAt(l) == s.charAt(r)) { count++; l--; r++; }
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

