**Pitfall:** Forgetting even-length centres undercounts `"aa"`-type palindromes.

**Key idea:** `ways(i)` = number of decodings of `s[i:]`. Take one digit if it isn't `0` (→ `ways(i+1)`), and also two digits if they form 10–26 (→ `ways(i+2)`). Same shape as Climbing Stairs, with validity checks.

**Brute force:** Recursively branch on one-digit / two-digit pieces with no caching. Time O(2ⁿ), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Top-down DP (memoized recursion)
`ways(n) = 1` (empty suffix). A `0` at position `i` gives 0 ways. Otherwise add `ways(i+1)`, plus `ways(i+2)` when `s[i..i+1]` is ≤ 26.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private String s;
    private Integer[] memo;

    public int numDecodings(String s) {
        this.s = s;
        memo = new Integer[s.length() + 1];
        return ways(0);
    }

    private int ways(int i) {
        if (i == s.length()) return 1;
        if (s.charAt(i) == '0') return 0; // no piece starts with 0
        if (memo[i] != null) return memo[i];
        int res = ways(i + 1);
        if (i + 1 < s.length() && Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i, i + 2)) <= 26) res += ways(i + 2);
        return memo[i] = res;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
