**Pitfall:** Updating `hi` first and then using the new `hi` to compute `lo`. Save the old values (or use a tuple assignment).

**Key idea:** `ok(i)` = "the suffix `s[i:]` can be segmented". It's true if some word matches at `i` and `ok(i + len(word))` holds. Only n + 1 distinct suffixes exist, so cache them.

**Brute force:** Recursively try every word at every position with no caching. Exponential on inputs like `"aaaa…ab"`. Time O(2ⁿ), Space O(n).

:::details 1. Top-down DP (memoized recursion)
`ok(n) = true`. For position `i`, try each dictionary word that starts there; cache the result per `i`.

**Java**

```java
class Solution {
    private String s;
    private List<String> words;
    private Boolean[] memo;

    public boolean wordBreak(String s, List<String> wordDict) {
        this.s = s;
        words = wordDict;
        memo = new Boolean[s.length() + 1];
        return ok(0);
    }

    private boolean ok(int i) {
        if (i == s.length()) return true;
        if (memo[i] != null) return memo[i];
        for (String w : words)
            if (s.startsWith(w, i) && ok(i + w.length())) return memo[i] = true;
        return memo[i] = false;
    }
}
```

**Python**

```python
